{% doc %}
  Records that a ticket just saw traffic, and keeps the search column in
  step with the subject and contact.

  Also the one place that knows which way a message went, so it is the one
  place that decides whether the ticket is waiting on us. An inbound email
  means the customer is waiting; an outbound reply means they are not. A
  private note goes through neither, which is right: writing a note to
  yourself does not answer anybody.

  last_customer_message_at is re-sent unchanged on an outbound touch rather
  than left out. record_update clears a property given an explicit null and
  a Liquid graphql call cannot drop an argument conditionally, so the value
  to keep has to be handed back in.

  @param {object} ticket - The ticket
  @param {string} direction - inbound or outbound
  @param {string} [contact_email] - For the search column
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  assign now = 'now' | date: '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'
  assign search = ticket.number | append: ' ' | append: ticket.subject | append: ' ' | append: contact_email | downcase

  assign needs_reply = false
  assign last_customer_message_at = ticket.last_customer_message_at
  if direction == 'inbound'
    assign needs_reply = true
    assign last_customer_message_at = now
  endif

  graphql updated = 'modules/support/tickets/touch', id: ticket.id, last_message_at: now, last_message_direction: direction, needs_reply: needs_reply, last_customer_message_at: last_customer_message_at, c__search: search
  return updated.record
%}
